1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,245
What if you were the unluckiest investor alive?

2
00:00:03,565 --> 00:00:10,438
You finally invest all your savings, and the very next month, the worst financial crisis in eighty years begins.

3
00:00:10,758 --> 00:00:13,231
Within a year and a half, half your money is gone.

4
00:00:13,551 --> 00:00:19,890
This really happened to millions of people in October two thousand seven. So today, we follow one of them to the end.

5
00:00:20,210 --> 00:00:23,543
And the ending might be the most surprising number in this whole series.

6
00:00:24,343 --> 00:00:30,217
October twenty oh seven. Grandma gives you ten thousand dollars. Do something smart with it, sweetheart.

7
00:00:30,537 --> 00:00:36,133
Everyone says the market is doing great. Stocks are at record highs. Your banker smiles a lot.

8
00:00:36,453 --> 00:00:42,200
So you put all ten thousand dollars into an S and P five hundred index fund. In one go.

9
00:00:42,520 --> 00:00:46,723
Without knowing it, you just bought at the top before the global financial crisis.

10
00:00:47,523 --> 00:00:50,449
And then it begins. Banks wobble. Then they fall.

11
00:00:50,769 --> 00:00:56,527
Every month, your ten thousand dollars gets smaller. Nine thousand. Seven thousand. Six.

12
00:00:56,847 --> 00:01:02,350
By March two thousand nine, the index's average price is fifty one percent below where you bought.

13
00:01:02,670 --> 00:01:06,351
Your ten thousand dollars is worth about four thousand nine hundred.

14
00:01:06,671 --> 00:01:09,991
Grandma's gift, cut in half, in seventeen months.

15
00:01:10,311 --> 00:01:16,766
You start checking the account every morning. Then every hour. Then you delete the app, reinstall it, and check again.

16
00:01:17,566 --> 00:01:20,480
To understand why selling felt so reasonable, remember what the world looked like in early two thousand nine.

17
00:01:20,800 --> 00:01:28,277
Every news channel showed red arrows. Huge companies were being rescued. People were losing jobs and homes.

18
00:01:28,597 --> 00:01:33,230
Nobody on television was saying, this is a great time to stay calm. It felt like the end.

19
00:01:33,550 --> 00:01:39,459
That's the thing about the bottom. It never looks like the bottom while you're in it. It looks like the beginning of something worse.

20
00:01:40,259 --> 00:01:45,878
And here, at the very bottom, the timeline splits.

21
00:01:46,198 --> 00:01:52,433
In timeline A, you can't take it anymore. You sell everything and keep the cash. Four thousand nine hundred dollars, safe forever.

22
00:01:52,753 --> 00:02:01,321
In timeline B, you're scared, but you do nothing. You leave it there and try not to look.

23
00:02:01,641 --> 00:02:06,030
Most people believe they'd be timeline B. In two thousand nine, a lot of real people chose timeline A.

24
00:02:06,350 --> 00:02:14,001
Not because they were stupid. Because watching half your savings disappear feels like the house is on fire, and every instinct says run.

25
00:02:14,801 --> 00:02:17,993
Timeline B waits. Two thousand nine. Twenty ten. Twenty eleven. Still under water.

26
00:02:18,313 --> 00:02:25,291
Friends say you should've put it in a savings account. Max says you should've bought gold. Max says a lot of things.

27
00:02:25,611 --> 00:02:33,390
In March twenty thirteen, five and a half years after you bought, the average price finally climbs back above the October twenty oh seven level.

28
00:02:33,710 --> 00:02:36,241
You're back to ten thousand dollars. Five and a half years to get back to zero.

29
00:02:37,041 --> 00:02:44,517
And then, nobody rings a bell. It just keeps climbing.

30
00:02:44,837 --> 00:02:51,188
Twenty sixteen. Twenty twenty, with a scary drop. Twenty twenty two, another one. Timeline B keeps not looking.

31
00:02:51,508 --> 00:02:58,788
By September twenty twenty six, the unluckiest investment of the decade is worth about forty nine thousand eight hundred dollars.

32
00:02:59,108 --> 00:03:01,789
Price only. Dividends would make it noticeably more.

33
00:03:02,109 --> 00:03:06,312
Bought at the worst possible moment, and still almost five times the money.

34
00:03:07,112 --> 00:03:15,309
Quick note, because it matters. All the numbers in this series use price only.

35
00:03:15,629 --> 00:03:20,958
But many of the companies inside an index also pay dividends. A small share of their profits, paid to the people who own the shares.

36
00:03:21,278 --> 00:03:28,116
In a typical index fund, those dividends get reinvested automatically and buy even more shares.

37
00:03:28,436 --> 00:03:34,578
We left them out to keep the math simple and conservative. So every real timeline B in this series would likely have ended up with more, not less.

38
00:03:34,898 --> 00:03:37,208
Boring money, quietly paying you to wait.

39
00:03:38,008 --> 00:03:40,562
And timeline A, who sold at the bottom? Still about four thousand nine hundred dollars, plus whatever the bank paid in interest.

40
00:03:40,882 --> 00:03:48,661
The crash didn't destroy timeline A's money. Selling did. The loss only became permanent the day it was locked in.

41
00:03:48,981 --> 00:03:55,657
Same day bought. Same crash. One ends with about forty nine thousand eight hundred, the other with about four thousand nine hundred.

42
00:03:56,457 --> 00:03:59,905
And then there's Max. Max invested ten thousand dollars on the same day. But Max has a plan.

43
00:04:00,225 --> 00:04:07,237
I'll sell before it gets worse, and buy back in at the bottom. Easy.

44
00:04:07,557 --> 00:04:15,174
In October twenty oh eight, Max sells. His ten thousand dollars is about six thousand three hundred. Then he waits for the perfect moment to get back in.

45
00:04:15,494 --> 00:04:24,131
Prices go up, and he waits for a dip. Prices dip, and he waits for a bigger dip. Years go by.

46
00:04:24,451 --> 00:04:28,410
Max finally buys back in January twenty twenty one. By September twenty twenty six, his ten thousand dollars is worth about twelve thousand seven hundred.

47
00:04:28,730 --> 00:04:34,083
Timeline B, who did absolutely nothing, has almost four times as much.

48
00:04:34,883 --> 00:04:37,681
Now, for fun, imagine timeline C. A version of you with a time machine, who buys at the exact bottom in March two thousand nine.

49
00:04:38,001 --> 00:04:42,285
That perfect investor turns ten thousand dollars into about one hundred and one thousand.

50
00:04:42,605 --> 00:04:49,211
Impressive. But nobody can do that on purpose. In March two thousand nine, it felt like the end of the world, not like a sale.

51
00:04:49,531 --> 00:04:55,138
The interesting part isn't the perfect investor. It's that the worst investor still did fine, by simply staying.

52
00:04:55,938 --> 00:04:59,955
And there's a fourth timeline, the boring one.

53
00:05:00,275 --> 00:05:05,337
Instead of a lump sum, you invest one hundred dollars every month starting in October twenty oh seven. Through the crash, through everything.

54
00:05:05,657 --> 00:05:10,385
During the crash, every hundred dollars buys more shares, because prices are low. Without trying, you buy cheap.

55
00:05:10,705 --> 00:05:20,063
By September twenty twenty six, you've put in twenty two thousand eight hundred dollars. And it's worth about eighty four thousand.

56
00:05:20,863 --> 00:05:23,672
One more version. What if, instead of putting all ten thousand in at once, you'd split it into twelve equal parts, one per month, starting in October twenty oh seven?

57
00:05:23,992 --> 00:05:31,051
You'd still be buying right into the crisis. But each month, as prices fall, your money buys a little more.

58
00:05:31,371 --> 00:05:38,360
By September twenty twenty six, that version has about fifty six thousand three hundred dollars. More than the all-at-once version.

59
00:05:38,680 --> 00:05:47,748
It's not always better. When markets rise steadily, investing all at once usually wins. But spreading it out makes bad timing hurt less, and it's much easier on the nerves.

60
00:05:48,548 --> 00:05:54,585
Now, the uncomfortable truth. It doesn't always go this fast.

61
00:05:54,905 --> 00:05:59,607
If you'd bought at the top in August two thousand, it took until May two thousand seven to get back. And then the next crash hit right away.

62
00:05:59,927 --> 00:06:01,738
And the scariest one: September nineteen twenty nine. The average price fell about eighty five percent by June nineteen thirty two.

63
00:06:02,058 --> 00:06:07,805
Counting price only, it took until nineteen fifty four to get back. Twenty five years. Dividends made the real recovery faster, but it was still brutal.

64
00:06:08,125 --> 00:06:14,672
So staying calm only works if the money can stay for a long time.

65
00:06:15,472 --> 00:06:19,687
So what does the unluckiest investor teach us?

66
00:06:20,007 --> 00:06:25,440
One. Timing is mostly luck. Time in the market is what you actually control.

67
00:06:25,760 --> 00:06:30,625
Two. A crash only becomes a permanent loss when you sell. So only invest money that can stay put for many years.

68
00:06:30,945 --> 00:06:36,564
Three. Investing a fixed amount every month takes the guessing out, and buys more when prices are low.

69
00:06:36,884 --> 00:06:43,548
Four. Spread your risk. One country, one sector or one company can have its own nineteen twenty nine.

70
00:06:44,348 --> 00:06:47,111
In twenty twenty six, you visit Grandma and tell her the whole story.

71
00:06:47,431 --> 00:06:54,351
The crash, the panic, the five years of waiting, and the almost fifty thousand dollars.

72
00:06:54,671 --> 00:07:02,008
She nods and says: I told you to do something smart with it. I never said fast.

73
00:07:02,328 --> 00:07:09,724
Then she asks if you'd like to hear how she lived through the nineteen seventies. You say yes. It takes three hours. Totally worth it.

74
00:07:10,524 --> 00:07:16,398
And the honest part. This is not financial advice.

75
00:07:16,718 --> 00:07:21,595
These are real past prices, but there's no promise the future looks like the past. Some crashes take decades to recover.

76
00:07:21,915 --> 00:07:23,691
Fees, taxes and dividends aren't included. Think about your own time horizon and risk before you invest.

77
00:07:24,491 --> 00:07:30,668
The worst possible day to invest. And still, almost five times the money, for the one who didn't run.

78
00:07:30,988 --> 00:07:35,573
Tell me in the comments: in March two thousand nine, would you have sold, or held?

79
00:07:35,893 --> 00:07:37,600
And I'll see you in the other timeline.

